etely their decision that's  their sovereign right but it doesn't  exempt United States citizens from ICC  jurisdiction when crimes are committed  on the territory of the state party  among other places the ICC is now  looking into atrocities in Afghanistan  the case has focused on events after  2003 and alleged crimes by all sides  let's start with the Taliban  in 2001 the Taliban was in power they  were seen as an oppressive regime that  refused to hand over Osama bin Laden  after the September 11 attacks the u.s.  went into Afghanistan to push them out  by 2003 a new Afghan government was in  place and the US and NATO had control of  the military but Taliban fighters didn't  go away they and other militant groups  went on to wage a guerilla war not only  targeting Afghan forces and their NATO  allies but civilians too and the  violence hasn't stopped there are  attacks nearly every day in many parts  of Afghanistan the ICC is also looking  at Afghan forces and whether they  committed war crimes on a list of  accusations torture and sexual abuse of  detainees the court has its sights on  people like General Abdul Rashid Dostum  who until recently was Afghanistan's  vice-president during the war he led a  pro-government militia whose fighters  are accused of atrocities like killing  and burying thousands of Taliban  prisoners and mass graves at the time  his militia was backed by US Special  Forces  that's them writing with general  Dawson's men which brings us to the  United States and what it stands accused  of for nearly 13 years Americans were  the major foreign power in Afghanistan  leading an international coalition that  at one point in 2010 reached a hundred  and fifty thousand foreign soldiers US  Special Forces were mainly there to  support the Afghan government but  they're accused of crimes including the  abuse of prisoners and covering up  killings  we are talking about use forces in my  downward AK D were abducting people  torturing them killing them 